The '''Treaty of Tamsui of 1656''' was the agreement that ended the [[Sinchew War]] and settled the future of [[Hachinawa]]. It was signed at [[Tamsui]] on 18 March 1656 by the king of Hachinawa, [[Kayatsi]], by the governor of the [[Sinchew Colony]], [[Ng Tek-seng]], and by the governor of the [[Louyang Colony]], [[Tân Chì-hong]].

The treaty divided Hachinawa in two along the [[Great Lurandian Mountain Range]]. The western territories were merged into the Sinchew Colony as a semi-autonomous province; the eastern territories remained independent under a protectorate. Its object was to preserve a Hachinawa state rather than to dissolve one, and it was made to prevent the empire's annexation by Louyang.

It formalised the modern boundaries of [[Sinchew]], except for the territory annexed in the [[Second War of the Isthmus]] of 1899, which in 1656 lay within the [[Viceroyalty of Balboa]] and was claimed by [[Volisania]].

== Background ==

By 1656 the war had ended without the defeat of any party. The Baiduist revolutionary army had been reduced in the colony's territory, Louyang had taken the whole of Hachinawa's southern frontier, and the king of Hachinawa, who had been concealed at Tamsui since 1648, had been brought out of concealment and into the negotiations in 1655.

Three parties had to agree on the empire's future, and each had a different object. Louyang held the territory it had taken and sought the remainder. Sinchew held the king and sought a settlement that would keep Louyang out of the empire's western districts. Hachinawa had no army in the field and no capital, and its king had nothing to negotiate with but the fact of his presence and the recognition he had received from the colony.

The treaty was made to prevent annexation. A restoration of Hachinawa as an independent state over its former territory would have left Louyang's army in possession of the south and given Louyang a footing from which to take the rest. The division of the empire between the colony and a reduced state of its own was the arrangement by which a Hachinawa polity survived at all.

== Terms ==

=== Division ===

The empire was divided along the [[Great Lurandian Mountain Range]], the north–south range whose western flank forms Sinchew's eastern highlands. The territories west of the range were merged into the Sinchew Colony. Those east of it remained independent.

=== Western province ===

The western territories were constituted as a semi-autonomous province of the Sinchew Colony under the following terms:

* The administrator. Kayatsi governed the province for his own lifetime. On his death the administrator was to be selected by the governor of Sinchew.
* The assembly. The assembly of Hachinawa was permitted to remain in being and to exercise legislative authority within the province.
* The veto. The provincial administrator held a veto over the assembly's legislation.

The province therefore retained the institutions of the empire, and the administrator's veto placed him above the assembly in a state whose kings had been elected by that assembly until the sixteenth century.

=== Southern territories ===

The southern territories of Hachinawa, which Louyang's land force had taken in 1648 and held for the remainder of the war, were confirmed to the [[Louyang Colony]]. The treaty recognised Louyang's possession of them, and the boundary between the colony's new southern territory and the empire's eastern half was fixed with it.

=== Baiduist population ===

The treaty provided for the removal of the Baiduist population of the colony. About 50,000 Baiduists were executed or displaced in the settlement, and the displacement was concentrated in the districts along [[Tn̂g-ô͘]] where the revolutionary army had been raised.

=== Eastern protectorate ===

The eastern territories remained independent and were constituted as a protectorate of [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]] for the lifetime of Hachatsi, the general who had commanded the empire's army in the war. On his death the protectorate was to end, and the territory was to pass to the royal house, which took the name [[Etowah]].

The eastern half comprised the empire's highlands and the Aethiopean coast, and the royal family had withdrawn to it in 1648.

== Signing and ratification ==

The treaty was signed by the three parties at Tamsui on 18 March 1656. It was ratified by the [[General Assembly]] of Zong and signed by the emperor on 1 June 1656, and it came into effect on 1 September of that year.

The interval of two and a half months between signature and ratification, and of three months between ratification and effect, reflect the distances involved: the instrument had to travel from Tamsui to the Zong court and back.

== Omissions ==

The treaty names three parties and assumes that there are three. It makes no reference to [[Free Sinchew]], which had been established north-east of Tamsui in the last years of the war, nor to the territories of the [[Yelani]] chiefdoms. Both lay within the region the treaty was dividing, and neither was a party to it.

The treaty was made between the empire, the colony and Louyang, and it disposed of the empire's territory among them. Free Sinchew and the Yelani held ground the three parties did not possess, and the treaty did not address it. Free Sinchew was conquered by the Sinchew Colony in 1715.

== Legacy ==

The treaty fixed the boundary that became the modern frontier of Sinchew. The two halves of Hachinawa were divided along the range, and the western province passed into the colony with its institutions intact.

In Zong the settlement was regarded as a success: it secured all the territory then under Zong control except the ground held by Free Sinchew, and it did so at the expense of Louyang, whose claim to the whole empire was refused.

